    <h2>Dimension 5: Total Cost of Ownership (5-Year View)</h2>

    <p>
      "EVs are cheaper to run" is a half-truth that needs context. The full
      picture includes purchase price, depreciation, fuel, maintenance,
      insurance, and road tax. For a typical Malaysian C-segment SUV, here is a
      rough 5-year cost comparison for a 20,000 km/year driver.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Assumptions:</strong> RON95 at RM 2.05/L (subsidised rate for eligible
      Malaysians), home wallbox electricity at RM 0.50/kWh blended, public DC fast
      charging at RM 1.50/kWh, road tax based on engine capacity for ICE/HEV and the
      lower EV schedule, residuals from industry-average Malaysian second-hand market
      data (not optimistic showroom talk).
    </p>

    <p>
      The chosen models: Honda CR-V 1.5L turbo for ICE, Toyota Corolla Cross
      Hybrid for HEV, and BYD Sealion 6 DM-i or Jaecoo J7 PHEV for PHEV (both
      are real C-segment PHEVs in this price range — the Jaecoo J7 PHEV launched
      at RM 158,800 with a 106 km EV range, the Sealion 6 DM-i at a similar
      price point with ~92 km). There is no Proton X70 PHEV, despite what some
      listings claim.
    </p>

